# Important Correction

For #6, your starting value and update are mixed.

You wrote:

```ruby
lowest_price_item = items[0][:price]
```

That stores only the price number.

But later you do:

```ruby
lowest_price_item = item
```

That changes it into the whole hash.

Better:

```ruby
lowest_price_item = items[0]

items.each do |item|
  if item[:price] < lowest_price_item[:price]
    lowest_price_item = item
  end
end

p lowest_price_item
```

Output:

```ruby
{:name=>"shoes", :price=>150}
```

# Clean Ruby Version Later

Ruby has built-in methods for this:

```ruby
numbers.sum
numbers.min
numbers.max
```

And for hashes:

```ruby
items.min_by { |item| item[:price] }
items.min_by { |item| item[:name].length }
```

But learning the long way matters because it shows what Ruby is doing underneath.

